  -> dawn
?end
-> :night

# dawn
## The black shell
The morning of the twenty-ninth is bright and clean and very still. It is the finest day of the spring. There is no wind. The smoke goes straight up from the faubourg in a single gray column, and the whole of the east of the city smells like a cooled hearth. The works of Réveillon are a black shell, with the sky showing through the beams, and the street in front of them has been washed. It was done at dawn, with buckets, by the soldiers. There is a very great deal of straw.
Nobody will agree on the number of the dead. The court says a dozen. The faubourg says a thousand. The people who carried them out say that it was more than the first number and a good deal less than the second, and that it does not make any difference, because each of them had a name.
The Estates General are to open at Versailles in a week. The king is going to receive the deputies of the three orders in his palace, in procession, with banners, with bishops and nobles and six hundred members of the Third in plain black coats, and it is the thing that the whole of France has been waiting for, for a hundred and seventy five years. It is going to be a very fine procession. There is going to be a good deal of talk, after the riot, about whether it should go ahead.
There is a knock at the shop door at a little after seven.
It is not the police. It is Séverin, and Clémence beside him, and a pale boy behind them with his cap in his hand and a darkened place on the throat of his shirt. They have not slept. They have been walking. Séverin has the plain gray coat on, and is not smiling, and has the look of a man who has been very much a boy until quite lately and has had to give it up.
"We are not asking," he says. He does not say it unkindly. He says it as one reports the weather. "We came to tell you. It is going to be the summer, and it is going to be worse than this, and we are going to be in it. I thought that you would like to know it before you were told it by somebody else."
Clémence says nothing. She looks past you at the shop, at the stair, at the shutter that is still on the counter with a brown stain on it, and at the girl with the bandaged shoulder who is asleep on a pile of folded paper. She has her hand on the door frame, and it is a very small hand, and there is white dust in the creases of it, and there always will be.
?if b3.weapon == "accepted"
  There is a folded note in your coat, in the Prince's hand, that you have not yet answered.
?end
?if b3.galerius == "senate"
  You think of a room in the rue des Prouvaires, over a chandler's, with a single iron stove, and of an old man who will have heard the volley from there, and have counted.
?end
$ do rest()
